@page "/components/shift-entity-form"

@using ShiftSoftware.ShiftBlazor.Components

<DocPageMeta Title="ShiftEntityForm"
             Section="Components"
             Order="20"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Draft"
             Description="Load, create, edit and delete one Shift Entity record from its endpoint, with modes, validation, permissions and the form context."
             Keywords="ShiftEntityForm,form,crud,edit,create,view,mode,context,FormChildContext,TypeAuthAction,OnSaveAction,ShiftForm,TheItem" />

<DocSection Title="What ShiftEntityForm does">
    <p>
        <code>ShiftEntityForm</code> is a form bound to <b>one record</b> of a Shift Entity endpoint.
        You give it the endpoint and, optionally, the record's key; it does the HTTP work and draws the
        header, toolbar and Save button around the fields you put inside it.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>With a <code>Key</code></b> it loads the record with <code>GET {Endpoint}/{Key}</code> and opens in <b>View</b> mode.</li>
        <li><b>Without a <code>Key</code></b> it switches itself to <b>Create</b> mode and saves with <code>POST {Endpoint}</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Edit</b> then <b>Save</b> sends <code>PUT {Endpoint}/{Key}</code> with the form's current item as the body.</li>
        <li><b>Delete</b> sends <code>DELETE {Endpoint}/{Key}</code>.</li>
        <li><b>View Revisions</b> appears once a record is loaded and the user has Read access. <code>HideRevisions</code> hides it.</li>
        <li><b>Print</b> is <b>hidden by default</b>: set <code>ShowPrint="true"</code> to show it (the user also needs Read
            access). It works in View and Archive mode only. It gets a token with <code>GET {Endpoint}/print-token/{Key}</code>,
            then opens <code>{Endpoint}/print/{Key}</code> with that token in a new browser tab, so the API must serve both
            routes. With <code>PrintConfig</code> set, it first opens a dialog where the user picks print options.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <code>Endpoint</code> is required: the form throws <code>ArgumentNullException</code> without it. The URL is
        the configured base address (or <code>BaseUrl</code> / <code>BaseUrlKey</code>) plus <code>Endpoint</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note" Title="Live examples">
        The examples on this page run against this site's demo API. Its data lives in memory and is shared by
        everyone reading the page, and it resets when the site restarts.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocComponentVariant Title="Load and edit a record" Linehighlight="3-7,10-12" DocComponent="@typeof(ShiftEntityFormBasicUse)">
    <p class="mt-4">
        Bind the item with <code>@@bind-Value</code> and the key with <code>@@bind-Key</code>, set the
        <code>Endpoint</code>, and place the fields inside the form. The fields read and write
        <code>context.Item</code>: the record this form loaded. The form opens in View mode (read-only);
        press <b>Edit</b> to change the name, then <b>Save</b>.
    </p>
</DocComponentVariant>

<DocComponentVariant Title="Create a record" Linehighlight="8" DocComponent="@typeof(ShiftEntityFormCreate)">
    <p class="mt-4">
        Leave out the key and the form opens in <b>Create</b> mode. Validation comes from the DTO's data
        annotations: <code>Name</code> and <code>AvatarIcon</code> are <code>[Required]</code> on
        <code>CustomerDTO</code>, so the <b>Create</b> button (the Save button in Create mode) shows an error on
        each empty field and sends nothing until both are filled.
    </p>
    <p>
        What happens after a successful save is decided by <code>OnSaveAction</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>ViewFormOnSave</code> (the default): shows a success message, switches to View mode and shows the saved record.
            After a <b>create</b> on a routed page it also appends the new key to the browser URL, so the page's route
            should accept it (for example <code>/CustomerForm/{Key?}</code>). Inside a modal it updates the modal's key instead.</li>
        <li><code>ResetFormOnSave</code>: clears the form to a new item, ready for the next one, without a success message.
            This example uses it, because this page has no route for the new key.</li>
        <li><code>CloseFormOnSave</code>: <b>only for a form open in a modal</b>. It closes the modal and returns the saved item
            as the modal's result, without a success message. On a form that is not in a modal it does nothing after the
            save: no message, and the form stays in Edit or Create mode.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="The user's setting wins">
        The form reads <code>SettingManager.Settings.FormOnSaveAction</code> first, then the
        <code>OnSaveAction</code> parameter, then the default. A user who chose a save action in their settings
        overrides the parameter.
    </DocCallout>
</DocComponentVariant>

<DocSection Title="Modes">
    <p>The form is always in one of four <code>FormModes</code>:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>View</code>: showing a loaded record, read-only.</li>
        <li><code>Archive</code>: showing a past revision, read-only.</li>
        <li><code>Edit</code>: changing a loaded record.</li>
        <li><code>Create</code>: entering a new record.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <code>ReadOnly</code> means the mode is below <code>Edit</code>, that is View or Archive. You can pass
        <code>Mode</code> in and follow its changes with <code>@@bind-Mode</code>.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocComponentVariant Title="Use the form context" Linehighlight="9-12,18-21" DocComponent="@typeof(ShiftEntityFormContext)">
    <p class="mt-4">
        Everything inside the form receives a <code>context</code> of type
        <code>FormChildContext&lt;T&gt;</code> that describes <b>this</b> form:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>context.Item</code>: the record the form holds (its <code>Value</code>).</li>
        <li><code>context.Mode</code>, <code>context.ReadOnly</code>, <code>context.Disabled</code>: the form's mode, whether it is read-only, and whether a task such as saving is in progress.</li>
        <li><code>context.Embedded</code>: the form's <code>Embedded</code> parameter. A host that shows a form as a pane
            inside something else sets it, so the form doesn't take over the page: it doesn't switch itself to Create
            mode, register keyboard shortcuts, autofocus, open a revision from the URL or join the record's attention
            feed. It does <b>not</b> make the form read-only: the form keeps the <code>Mode</code> it is given. The
            revision compare view, for example, passes <code>Embedded="true"</code> together with <code>Mode="FormModes.Archive"</code>.</li>
        <li><code>context.SetMode(...)</code>, <code>context.Validate()</code>, <code>context.ShowAlert(...)</code>, <code>context.CloseModal()</code>: act on the form from your fields.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Press <b>Edit</b> in this example: the chips and the extra line follow the form's own mode.
    </p>
</DocComponentVariant>

<DocSection Title="Permissions">
    <p>
        Set <code>TypeAuthAction</code> to the entity's TypeAuth action (a <code>ReadWriteDeleteAction</code>).
        The form then checks the signed-in user's access: without Read it does not load the record, without Write
        the Edit button is hidden, and without Delete the Delete button is hidden.
        <code>TypeAuthAction="null"</code>, as in the examples above, allows everything.
    </p>
    <DocCodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@(@"<ShiftEntityForm @bind-Value=""_item""
                 @bind-Key=""Key""
                 Endpoint=""Vehicle""
                 TypeAuthAction=""StockPlusPlusActionTree.Vehicle"">
    ...
</ShiftEntityForm>")" />
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Danger" Title="Hiding a button is not security">
        <code>TypeAuthAction</code> only changes what the form shows. Anyone can still call the API directly, so the
        controller must enforce the same action: pass it to the secure controller's base constructor
        (<code>base(StockPlusPlusActionTree.Vehicle)</code>). With <code>base(null)</code> every API check is skipped.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Migrating from ShiftForm">
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="@("ShiftForm<TComponent, T> is obsolete")">
        Older pages inherit <code>ShiftForm&lt;TComponent, T&gt;</code> and bind the form to the page's
        <code>TheItem</code>, <code>Mode</code> and <code>FormContainer</code>. A page has only one of each, so two
        forms on the same page share one record: both show whichever loaded last, Edit in one switches both, and
        saving one can write the other record under its own key. <code>ShiftForm</code> also drops cascading
        parameters, which includes <code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</code>.
    </DocCallout>
    <p>To migrate a page:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Remove the <code>@@inherits ShiftForm&lt;...&gt;</code> line.</li>
        <li>Add <code>[Parameter] public object? Key { get; set; }</code> and a private field for the item.</li>
        <li>Bind them on the form with <code>@@bind-Key</code> and <code>@@bind-Value</code>.</li>
        <li>Replace <code>TheItem.X</code>, <code>Mode</code>, <code>ReadOnly</code> and <code>Disabled</code> with
            <code>context.Item.X</code>, <code>context.Mode</code>, <code>context.ReadOnly</code> and <code>context.Disabled</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@(@"@* Before *@
@inherits ShiftForm<CustomerForm, CustomerDTO>

<ShiftEntityForm @bind-Value=""TheItem"" @bind-Mode=""Mode"" @ref=""FormContainer"" Endpoint=""Customer"">
    <MudTextFieldExtended @bind-Value=""TheItem.Name"" For=""@(() => TheItem.Name)"" />
</ShiftEntityForm>

@* After *@
<ShiftEntityForm @bind-Value=""_item"" @bind-Key=""Key"" Endpoint=""Customer"">
    <MudTextFieldExtended @bind-Value=""context.Item.Name"" For=""@(() => context.Item.Name)"" />
</ShiftEntityForm>

@code {
    [Parameter] public object? Key { get; set; }
    private CustomerDTO _item = new();
}")" />
</DocSection>

